-- title:
--   p. 234 — $T$ consists exactly of its piecewise linear circle maps
-- statement:
--   A permutation $f$ of the circle lies in $T$ if and only if it satisfies `IsThompsonCircle`: it lifts to an order isomorphism $L$ of $\mathbb{R}$ commuting with translation by $1$, mapping dyadic rationals to dyadic rationals, and piecewise affine with slopes powers of $2$ and finitely many dyadic breakpoints modulo $1$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $T$ is defined as the subgroup generated by these maps, so the theorem says that they are closed under composition and inversion and include the identity: the source's "it is easy to see that $T$ is indeed a group".
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) 42 (1996) 215–256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, section 5, p. 234, the definition of T

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry_T

namespace CannonFloydParry

theorem mem_T_iff_isThompsonCircle {f : Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle} :
    f ∈ T ↔ IsThompsonCircle f := by
  sorry

